# row that feels along a boundary and presses each post to see whether it still stands. The note
# above names the instrument that was missing in its own words: an aggregate ratchet answers "how
# many runners does no lap reach", and cannot answer "is the tool this law page declares as its
# checkable surface one of them". Three tools were seated by hand that day and the answer became
# yes; nothing since then has been able to tell whether it stayed yes. A row added to the table
# tomorrow can be unrostered exactly as those three were, and every standing guard reads green --
# which is a fence post firm to the eye and loose to the hand.
#
# `tame_core_surface` asks that second question directly, three walls at zero over the NINE tools
# `context/TAME_CORE.md` names today: each exists on disk, each carries a `path` row here, and each
# declared Clock matches the roster's own `tier`. A tier absent reads `lap`, which is this file's
# own rule rather than a convenience. Measured at the seating: 9 declared, 281 roster guards, all
# three readings zero -- the hand repair of `20260908.111848` held, and is held now by construction.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT READ, said plainly so a green never reads wider than it is: whether a tool reads
# what its Reads column claims, whether the table names every tool it ought to, and whether a
# rostered guard is green. Those are three other questions with three other instruments. This one
# reads the page's own declaration against the roster -- the claim, pressed where the claim is made.
