# spoken_ascii -- the ASCII-first law names three subjects, and the tree's guards speak in a fourth.
# Both comment meters refuse to count "program content", and `ascii_comment_witness` gives the
# reason in its own GREEN line: converting a string "changes what a program prints". That one
# exclusion answers two questions. A heredoc body is what a program feeds ONWARD, and converting it
# changes behavior -- rightly excluded, here as there. A `say` line is what a program says TO A
# PERSON, on their terminal and into `session-output/`, and converting it changes register, which is
# what the law governs. Measured 20260907.075500 over the same 2,387 `.rish` sources the comment
# meter opens, under the same law, on the same run: 505 characters counted and held, against 11,154
# counted by nothing -- and 11,154 is very nearly the 10,468 the comment surface itself stood at on
# the day its own meter was built. Same files, same law, one sweep less of attention. `tier cadence`:
# 10s, and it reads 2,387 files where a lap touches a handful.
guard spoken_ascii
path tools/s/spoken_ascii_witness.rish
seated 20260907.075500
tier cadence   # non-ASCII in what a Rishi guard says out loud, a ratchet that only falls

# rye_spoken_ascii -- the sibling above proved the argument and stopped at one language. A heredoc
# body is what a program feeds ONWARD; a `say` line is what it says TO A PERSON, and converting that
# changes register, which is what the ASCII-first law governs. Rye speaks through `print`, and the
# Rye comment meter declines program content for that same one reason -- so the tree held a guard
# over what a Rishi program says and none over what a Rye program says, in the language it writes
# most of its own modules in. Measured 20260908.214712 over the same 1,730 tracked `.rye` sources
# the comment meter opens: 3,794 characters counted and held there, against 3,996 counted by
# nothing, in 809 of those files -- 45% of the tree's authored Rye printing a character to a
# person's terminal that no instrument read. A LINE-ORIENTED READING WOULD HAVE MISSED HALF ITS OWN
# SUBJECT: this tree writes a claim line as a chain of literals joined by `++` across four or five
# lines, and 2,480 characters stand on lines holding the call against 2,235 on its continuations, so
# the scan reads parenthesis depth outside string literals. The seating lap swept its own lane to
# zero -- mantra and tally, 25 files, 101 characters -- and set the ceiling at what remained.
# `tier cadence`: it reads 1,730 files where a lap touches a handful.
guard rye_spoken_ascii
path tools/r/rye_spoken_ascii_witness.rish
seated 20260908.214712
