# The elder record, lifted rather than converted. `mantra/src/main.rye` writes a three-field
# `mantra-weave-v1` blob and this module widened past it, so the open question (REDS %589) was
# whether closing that gap breaks the record already on disk. It does not: the record was
# written by one hand, so site and run are CONSTANT across it rather than absent from it, and a
# constant run beside a constant site reduces `Place.less_than` to `pos <` -- the elder reader's
# own comparison. `Weave.from_v1` is that sentence as code; the witness compares the two readers
# line for line and proves four refusals the elder reader never had. It ports nothing and moves
# no byte on disk; it makes the port mechanical and the record's safety checkable.
guard mantra_weave_v1_lift
path tools/m/mantra_weave_v1_lift_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260908.051232

# The elder record, written back. The lift guard above answered the READING half of REDS %589:
# an elder `mantra-weave-v1` record reads the same after the widening, since site and run are
# constant across a record one hand wrote. `mantra/src/main.rye` WRITES that record on every
# commit, so until a weave the module has advanced can be written back as three fields, "the
# port moves no byte on disk" was a claim about reading wearing the clothes of a claim about
# the record. `Weave.to_v1` is the other half, and the round trip is identity on the DOCUMENT
# rather than on the weave -- the runs come back collapsed to `v1_run`, and the document is
# unmoved because inside ONE site `next_run` and `next_pos` both only rise at the same apply,
# so a higher run always carries a higher position. What the format cannot hold it refuses by
# name: a second site, since the record has no field to tell two hands apart; and a merge whose
# position order has parted from its document order, which the witness BUILDS rather than
# argues. It ports nothing and moves no byte on disk.
guard mantra_weave_v1_write
path tools/m/mantra_weave_v1_write_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260908.123338

# The record a merged history fits in. The two guards above named this gap in their own headers
# and neither could close it: `to_v1` refuses a second site by name, and a second site is exactly
# and only what `merge` produces -- so Mantra could merge two histories and had nowhere to put
# the result, since `mantra/src/main.rye` writes the elder record on every commit. `Weave.to_v2`
# and `Weave.from_v2` are the five-field record, and the round trip is identity on the WEAVE
# rather than only on the document: the runs come back as they went. THE COUNTERS ARE CARRIED
# AND DID NOT HAVE TO BE, which this round measured rather than argued -- a first draft asserted
# they cannot be derived, and they can, since `merge` maxes two counters while dropping no line,
# so the side holding the higher counter also contributes the line below it. Claim 3 measures
# that equality on a lifted, an edited and a merged weave; the record carries the two numbers
# anyway because the weave's DECLARED invariant is an upper bound rather than an equality, and
# claim 4 holds a record standing above the accident. Four refusals, three of which an elder
# record could not get wrong. WHAT IS NOT PROVEN: that the CLI writes this record -- it still
# serializes v1, so the format has no writer yet, which is the next lap and its own round for the
# reason the lift and the write were two. `tier lap` beside its sibling at 14.4s: the control
# builds sixteen pens for 20.9s measured under a contended pier, the same order rather than a
# new one.
guard mantra_weave_v2
path tools/m/mantra_weave_v2_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260909.233534

