# mantra_idempotent_add -- a second `mantra add` over a file nobody edited changes nothing. The
# weave is content-addressed and `commit_files_with` returned the prior list entry for entry, yet
# `cmd_add` wrote a commit blob and moved HEAD on every run, so the record grew with the polling
# rather than with the work; `mantra log` walks at most 1,000 commits, so a watcher calling `add`
# on a timer pushes the real first commit past the end of the log inside a day. `files_agree`
# compares the two lists and `cmd_add` returns without writing when they agree. The scan hashes
# every byte under `.mantra/` after three runs, and reads the opposite direction too -- a real edit
# and a new path must still land -- because a skip is as wrong when it fires too eagerly.
guard mantra_idempotent_add
path tools/m/mantra_idempotent_add_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260910.024500

# rish_report_bound -- a guard's success report has a ceiling and its refusal does not, and until
# 20260907 nothing read the difference. Rishi composes an interpolated string literal through
# `StrBuf`, a bounded 4,096-byte buffer in rishi/src/main.rye; a bare `say <value>` writes straight
# out under no ceiling. `reds_spine_derive_witness.rish` passed BOTH its gates -- `verdict=ok`,
# `rebindings=0` -- and then ended the run at `say "label ${scan.out}"`, because the reading had
# grown to 4,250 bytes as the ledger grew. Eight ships paid a red every lap for a green reading,
# and the message named the guard's own reporting line rather than the tree. The asymmetry is
# deliberate on Rishi's side and is the finding: an `assert ... else` message that overflows falls
# back to its raw literal, so the REFUSAL path degrades rather than dies -- the unprotected path is
# the one that fires when everything is right. Ratchet at 38 sites across 26 files, never a gate:
# every site is correct today and fails only when its own reading grows, so a gate would refuse
# ordinary work. Narrowed from 504 interpolating sites across 467 files by requiring the file to
# read the same capture with `contains` -- a verdict-bearing report rather than a probe's one line;
# counting the rest would push 466 files toward worse prose to reach the 26 carrying the hazard.
# Three claims proven against the real interpreter on metal in the control, not read out of source.
# `lap` rather than `cadence`: the elder lap measured 19s and this pier re-proved it at 31.7s on
# the day it was rostered, and the shape it counts arrives in any lap that writes a guard.
guard rish_report_bound
path tools/r/rish_report_bound_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260907.233349

# The name a blob carries, spent on every read. `mantra/src/store.rye` named a blob by its
# SHA3-256 digest and then never checked it again: `Sha3.hash` stood in `write_blob` alone, so
# the whole content-addressing claim rested on the bytes on disk never changing. Beside it the
# read used `Dir.readFile`, whose `readSliceShort` returns `buffer.len` the moment the buffer
# fills, so a blob past the ceiling came back as its own first megabyte with no error raised.
# Together those are silent corruption rather than two faults: a truncated `mantra-weave-v1`
# record is a VALID PREFIX, so it lifts as a shorter weave and the next commit writes it back
# under its own honest digest. Proven on metal before this row was written -- the elder CLI
# reported a diff against a truncated history and exited 0; the ported one refuses
# `BlobNameMismatch`. Four numeric ceilings stood unnamed across two files, two of them the
# same function at two heights (1 MiB here, 4 MiB in `main.rye`, over one `.mantra/blobs/`);
# `max_blob_bytes` is one number at 128 MiB, so folding them RAISED the shipped capacity --
# which is why the port was declined once already (`20260908.143034`). It builds two binaries
# and writes a 128 MiB file, so it is 21s and a lap guard rather than a cadence one.
guard mantra_store
path tools/m/mantra_store_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260908.191500

