# shim_reason -- a pass-through shim hands on its target's reason, not only its verdict.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG, measured on a real file with no plant. `tools/am/*.rish` and their kin are accrete
# shims: each runs a target under `tools/gen/` and exits that target's code, so either path may be
# named and the answer is the same. Rishi's `run` captures the target's stderr into `r.err`, and a
# shim that says only `r.out` writes nothing to its own stderr. `amphora_device_wire` refuses
# honestly on this pier -- it drives a virtio lab and there is no qemu here -- so the two readings
# stand side by side without breaking anything: the target run directly says `rishi: assertion
# failed -- Amphora vessel fetch device wire lab failed`, with the line number; the shim run the same
# second exits 1 with ZERO bytes on stderr, and all a reader receives is `amphora-device-wire:
# virtio fetch-by-digest lab...`, a progress line printed BEFORE the failure, which reads like
# success. This runner captures a guard with `> out 2>&1`, so the merge was already there and caught
# nothing: the shim emitted nothing to merge.
#
# A GATE AND A RATCHET, because this is a trap rather than a live fault. Zero swallowing shims stand
# on this roster, so nothing is lost today; it springs on the lap that ROSTERS one, and %360's
# standing pressure is to roster more. The amphora lane walked into it on `20260906.063124` -- three
# shims onto a lap clock, the swallow found by planting a break and repaired in the same breath. The
# gate is what spares the next lane that discovery. The ratchet is the residue off the roster, and it
# only falls; the scan's own `unrostered_ceiling` line carries the number so no page can drift from
# it. Nine of 52 shims forward the reason today, and five of those nine were repaired in this lap.
#
# THE GATE IS PROVEN ABLE TO BITE, which matters more than usual here: `swallow_rostered` reads zero
# on this field and always has, so nothing in the tree could show it working. The pen supplies the
# case -- one swallowing shim named by a `path` row, refused; the same shim with one line added,
# freed. The ceiling is proven from both sides on ONE plant, two unrostered swallowers read at a
# ceiling of two and then of one.
#
# THREE MARKS, TOGETHER. A shim is the `run ["rishi/bin/rishi" "run" ...]` call, the `say r.out`
# forward, and the `exit r.code` tail, all three -- each alone is ordinary, since witnesses run
# witnesses and plenty of scripts say a captured stdout. A seat is a `path` row at column zero,
# never a path mentioned in a roster comment; unanchored, a guard could count as rostered because
# somebody wrote about it, and the pen plants that sentence. Two tracked files exit a captured run's
# code under another variable name and are invisible to the reading; neither is a shim, and both are
# named every pass as `exit_alias_sites`, so the floor carries a number (%466).
#
# `tier lap`, and cheap because the corpus arrives through one `git grep` whose status is classified
# rather than swallowed (%473, %484). Three greps over 2,362 tracked `.rish` files cost 22 seconds
# measured on this pier at load 10.1; the pre-filter reads the identical counts in 2, and the whole
# guard including its 40-case pen runs in 5 against a full lap pass of 729.
#
# A SECOND SHAPE OF THE SAME LOSS, seated `20260906.233111` -- and found by a red rather than by a
# search. `fleet_watch` reddened a cold pass, filed seven lines that could not name a behavior, then
# passed 16 of 16 on six consecutive re-runs: the one reading that could have diagnosed the flake
# was the reading its witness threw away. `tools/fixtures/f/fleet_watch_control.sh:24` prints
# `FAIL <behavior> -- wanted X, got Y` to STDERR so a passing run stays quiet, and
# `tools/f/fleet_watch_witness.rish:17` interpolated `${ctl.out}` and never `${ctl.err}`. The exit
# code travelled, `pass=15 fail=1` travelled, and the clause naming WHICH behavior did not.
#
# Measured the same hour: EIGHT controls print FAIL to stderr, FIVE are run by a witness, and all
# five of those witnesses stood on this roster interpolating `.out` alone -- `fleet_watch`,
# `fleet_lap_verdict`, `publish_template`, `unshared_citation`, `sealed_digest`. All five carry
# `${ctl.err}` now, and the repair was proven on metal by planting one wrong expected value in
# `sealed_digest_control.sh`: the evidence went from `pass=8 fail=1` alone to that line plus
# `FAIL an unread seal is counted -- wanted PLANTED_WRONG, got yes`.
#
# THE THREE-MARK READING ABOVE COULD NOT SEE IT. That reading requires `say r.out` and
# `exit r.code`, which is a pass-through shim; these are witnesses that assert. One law -- hand on
# your target's reason -- and two shapes, so the instrument grew a second reading rather than the
# tree growing a second instrument. `reason_lost_rostered` is GATED at zero, `reason_lost_unrostered`
# is a ratchet opening at zero, and `stderr_controls` names the population so a zero is visible
# rather than assumed (%463).
#
# TWO REFUSALS IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT MAKE, each proven in the pen. A control keeping its FAIL
# line on STDOUT is not in the population at all -- its reason already travels through whatever the
# witness interpolates, and counting it would red a witness that is already correct. And a witness
# forwarding some OTHER run's stderr is not credited for the control's: the reading takes the
# variable the control's `run` is bound to and reads only that variable's spellings, because the
# blindest version of this check is *does the file mention `.err` anywhere*.
#
# AND THE READING CAUGHT ITSELF, which is the case worth keeping. The control's pen helper WRITES a
# stderr-reporting control, so its own source carries that line inside single quotes -- and a first
# pattern read 9 where the tree holds 8, counting the instrument as one of its own subjects. The
# population is anchored now: a real call stands after start-of-line or whitespace, a quoted mention
# stands after the quote that opens its string, and a comment is dropped in the same pass. Both
# spellings of a mention are planted, and the quoted plant reproduces the real continuation-line
# shape byte for byte rather than something near it.
#
# Twenty-one new pen readings, six new repositories, every refusal planted and then lifted: 61
# cases over 14 repositories, and the guard runs in 8 seconds measured `20260907.000030` against a
# full lap pass of 983.

guard shim_reason
