# reds_citation -- a number used as a link is a promise about where the link goes (REDS row of
# `20260906.143706`). A row is cited two ways at once, as a number a reader recognises and as a path
# a reader clicks, and the two halves move at different moments: the number when the derived spine
# renumbers an unshared row, the path when a row folds onto a shelf. Nothing read them together.
# Four citations went out in one day naming one row and linking another, and each repair fixed the
# half the eye was on -- the number moved and the path stayed, then the path moved and the number
# stayed.
#
# EXISTENCE AND AGREEMENT ARE TWO QUESTIONS, and only one had an instrument. Measured on the tree
# that morning, `readme_reach` named ONE of the four -- the citation whose file was absent -- and
# read green over the other three, which open a real shelf holding somebody else's row. That is a
# misdirection with no expiry: it resolves, so it never reds, and a reader following it lands
# somewhere the sentence never meant. `tracked_link` asks existence the same way; `reds_row_present`
# asks whether the ledger HOLDS a row and never asks who cites it.
#
# TWO FORMS, AND THE THIRTY-TWO THAT TAUGHT THE NARROWING. A window around a link picks up every
# `%N` in the neighbouring prose, and a sentence may lawfully name one row while linking another --
# %220's own shelf cites %218's. A nearest-preceding-%N reading over 1,001 living documents called
# 32 honest sentences wrong, so the scan reads only the two shapes that are promises to a reader:
# the anchor text that IS the number, and the shelf word whose sentence just named a row. One line
# of lookback, never past the link, because this tree hard-wraps its pins and the break that shipped
# fell exactly across that wrap.
#
# PROVEN ON HISTORY AS WELL AS IN A PEN. Run against `b441f97b2`, this tree as it stood when the
# defect was published, the scan reads `numbered_disagree=3` and names all three sites. The pen adds
# 23 cases over 12 real repositories, every refusal planted and then lifted, and it caught a defect
# in the scan's own counting before it shipped -- `grep -c ''` prints 0 and exits 1 on an empty file,
# so the fallback beside it yielded two lines and the emptiness branch was skipped entirely.
#
# `tier lap`: one `git grep` over tracked markdown then awk over the two dozen documents that cite a
# shelf, about a second. Every fold moves a path and every renumbering moves a number, so ordinary
# work is what writes this fault, and a cadence clock would let one ride four laps.

guard reds_citation
path tools/r/reds_citation_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260906.143706
# reds_shelf_name -- a fold shelf's own name is a promise about what it holds (REDS row of
# `20260907.151832`). A folded row spells its number FOUR times: in the shelf's filename, in its H1
# title, in a `**Rows:**` header, and in the row headline. `reds_spine_derive` is the only
# instrument in this tree that reads a row's number at all, and it reads the headline alone -- so a
# renumber sweep reaching three of the four leaves a shelf disagreeing with itself, and the two
# spellings it leaves behind are the filename, which 291 living citations open by path, and the
# title, which is the first line a reader sees.
#
# EXISTENCE IS NOT AGREEMENT, and this is that pair one room further in than `reds_citation`. That
# guard reads a citation's number against the shelf it links, from OUTSIDE the shelf; `readme_reach`
# and `tracked_link` ask whether a path opens, and a shelf whose title names the wrong row opens
# perfectly. Nothing read a shelf against itself. Measured `20260907.151832`: one shelf of 291
# disagreed, and it was this ship's own row from the lap before, renumbered `%568 -> %566 -> %572`
# on the anointed spine while its title kept `%568`.
#
# THE READING IS NARROW ON PURPOSE, and its three welcomes are proven as hard as its refusals. A
# title may name a row as its SUBJECT mid-sentence (*the %364 collision*); a `**Rows:**` header may
# recite a renumber history after its declaration; a filename span may be WIDER than the rows held,
# because a range is not a set and the endpoints folded elsewhere. All three stand in this archive
# today, and a looser reading calls every one of them wrong.
#
# `tier lap` because the derived spine renumbers unshared rows as routine work, so the fault is
# written by ordinary laps rather than by a deliberate act -- and the scan costs four seconds over
# `git ls-files` and 291 small files.
