# One literal, two verdicts, seated 20260909.001908. glow/rune_shop_gate.rye:522 carries a comment
# naming a "house parse law" -- refuses empty, leading zero, overflow -- and it is one of FOURTEEN
# source-decimal readers in the language, the only one holding it. Proven on metal the day this was
# seated, on three paths in one tree: `?:  (eq a b)  01  0` refuses with MalformedBody through the
# shop gate, while `=/  amount  007` lowers GREEN through glow/lower_multi.rye to
# `const amount: u32 = 7;` and `=/  amount  03` lowers GREEN through glow/lower_compose.rye to 3.
#
# The lexer is neutral, which is why the split could hide: glow/tokens.rye scan_digits takes any run
# of digits and emits one `.decimal` token, so nothing about a leading zero reaches the token stream.
# The disagreement lives entirely in the fourteen functions that turn those bytes into a u32, and no
# two of them are called from one place, so no test ever sat them side by side. Each is internally
# consistent and correct on its own terms; the fault exists only in the relation between them, which
# is how fourteen careful hands produced it.
#
# WHY THE RATCHET COUNTS VERDICTS RATHER THAN LAW-BREAKING READERS. Which way the law falls -- Glow
# refuses a leading zero everywhere, or accepts one everywhere -- is a language custody ruling that
# stays Keaton's. A ceiling on `law_absent` would presume it: rule that Glow accepts, and the repair
# deletes the check from its one holder, taking 13 to 14 and reddening this guard for landing the
# answer. The count of DISTINCT verdicts falls to one under either ruling, so the meter holds the
# fault still without deciding it. Two control legs carry exactly that shape -- a room refusing
# everywhere and a room accepting everywhere both read one verdict and both pass.
#
# `law_claimed_absent` is the gate, and it is the copy this split was made of.
# glow/rune_assert.rye parse_decimal is the shop gate's function byte for byte, minus the loop
# variable's name and minus the one line that refuses. Copying a law's shape without its check is
# how the split reached fourteen readers; copying its COMMENT too is the same move one step louder,
# decidable today, and held at zero.
#
# `tier lap` because it is file reading at 0.6s and the whole guard costs 4s, and because a ratchet
# only works if it is read every lap. The population is DERIVED from the room on every pass -- this
# lap's own first reading, taken by hand with grep, found 5 of the 14 (REDS %532's lesson landing on
# the hand that cited it).
guard glow_decimal_law
path tools/g/glow_decimal_law_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260909.001908

# query_wire_retention -- what a kept answer costs per read, measured rather than argued. The
# retention arc proved a decoded QueryWireResponse BORROWS its text from the payload, so a caller
# who keeps the answer keeps those bytes; it left unmeasured whether that caller should also keep
# the decoded struct. comlink/query_wire_retention_cost.rye brackets two workloads between reads of
# a self-scoped retired-instruction counter -- pid 0, no root, no `perf` binary -- alternating them
# sample by sample so host drift moves both together, and publishes the median of seventeen samples.
# WHY THE CONTROL CARRIES THE GATE: the cached-read loop is loop-invariant when it reads one fixed
# hit, and the harness's first draft read exactly that -- under -OReleaseFast twenty thousand reads
# compiled to six instructions and it published an elimination as a saving. The rotating hit index
# is the cure, and tools/fixtures/q/query_wire_retention_control.sh proves the cure load-bearing by
# planting the fault back and requiring the planted copy to collapse. A host refusing the counter
# says `tier=cpu_seconds` and passes free, since a host is not a fault. `tier lap` at a measured
# 1.5s on this pier with the builds cached: this is a performance claim, and a ship moves between
# machines, so the lap is where the figure gets checked against the metal under it.
guard query_wire_retention
path tools/q/query_wire_retention_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260909.152110

